  <artifactId>s06-synchronous-call-between-services</artifactId>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>s06-synchronous-call-between-services</name>
  <description>S6: when a request cannot continue without another service's answer.</description>

  <!--
    Two services, and again no shared contract module — but the reasoning is not S4's.

    Over a broker the argument is about durability: records outlive the code, so a shared jar forces a
    coordinated release on a contract that is already in flight. A synchronous HTTP call has no such
    backlog; both sides are live at the same moment. What a shared jar costs here is narrower and still
    real: the callee's DTOs become the caller's compile-time dependency, so every field the callee adds
    for someone else recompiles this caller, and the caller's model quietly inherits the callee's shape.

    So the caller hand-writes the two records it actually sends and reads, in its own client package,
    and the companion document argues the three options (shared jar, OpenAPI codegen, hand-written)
    rather than pretending there is only one.
  -->
  <modules>
    <module>risk-service</module>
    <module>ordering-service</module>
  </modules>
</project>
